{
  "id": "rules-in-the-wrong-order",
  "name": "Rules in the Wrong Order",
  "category": "security",
  "difficulty": 4,
  "xp": 350,
  "level": 9,
  "description": "The rule that allows web traffic is right there. It does nothing.",
  "brief": "🚨 NOTHING GETS OUT\n\nFirewall-01 has a rule that explicitly allows TCP/80. It is sitting in the list, spelled correctly, pointed the right way.\n\nWeb traffic is still being dropped.\n\nRules are read from the top and the first one that matches decides. Look at what sits above the allow — and at the hit counters, which show which rule is actually doing the work.",
  "hints": [
    "Run 'show access-list' on Firewall-01 and read it top to bottom.",
    "Which rule matches a TCP/80 packet first?",
    "The hit counters tell you which rule is really deciding.",
    "Move the allow rule above the broad deny with the arrow buttons."
  ],
  "explanation": "Order is not a detail in a rule list — it is the logic. Evaluation stops at the first match, so a broad 'deny tcp' above a specific 'allow tcp 80' means the allow is never even considered. The rule was present, correct, and completely inert.\n\nHit counters are the fastest way to see this: the rule doing the work is the one whose count climbs, not the one you expected to match.",
